          </div>

          <p>
            Older adults deserve a note. Aging muscle responds less to a given dose of protein, so leucine-rich, high-quality types such as whey and milk protein make it easier to reach the roughly 0.4 grams of protein per kilogram per meal that research suggests older adults need. Our guide to <a href="/muscle-protein-synthesis-supplements/">muscle protein synthesis supplements</a> covers the per-meal research. Anyone with kidney disease or on medication should agree on a protein target with a doctor or registered dietitian.
          </p>

          <p>
            For budget buyers, the order is usually whey concentrate, then soy isolate, then milk protein, with isolate, casein, pea blends, egg and beef costing progressively more per gram of protein, though prices shift with sales and flavors. Our <a href="/best-cheap-protein-powder/">best cheap protein powder</a> guide compares cost per gram among tested options.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="labels" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. How to Check What Type You're Actually Buying
          </h2>

          <p>
            The front of a tub says what the marketing wants you to see; the ingredient list tells you what is inside. Ingredients are listed in descending order by weight, so the first protein named makes up the largest share. A powder sold as whey isolate that lists whey protein concentrate first is mostly concentrate, with some isolate added.
          </p>

          <p>
            Watch for proprietary blends that list several proteins without amounts, which make it impossible to know how much of the expensive ingredient you are getting. Watch also for added free amino acids such as glycine, taurine or creatine listed near the protein, a practice called amino spiking that can inflate the protein grams on the label without adding real protein. A published amino acid profile, ideally showing leucine per serving, is a good sign.
          </p>

          <p>
            Check the protein per scoop against the scoop weight. A 30-gram scoop of isolate should deliver about 25 to 27 grams of protein. If a 40-gram scoop delivers 20 grams, the rest is filler, sugar or flavoring. Comparing protein per gram of powder is one of the quickest ways to spot a weak product, and our <a href="/protein-powder-comparison/">protein powder comparison</a> lines up popular products this way.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, look for third-party certification. NSF Certified for Sport and Informed Choice test that the product matches its label and screen for banned substances, which matters more than the protein type for most buyers. Once you know which type suits you, our <a href="/best-protein-powder/">best protein powder</a> rankings point you to tested products in each category.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Paying for hydrolysate you don't need</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Whey hydrolysate costs more and often tastes bitter, and for healthy adults there is little evidence it builds more muscle than isolate. Unless you have a sensitive gut or a medical reason, isolate or concentrate gives you the same results for less money.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using collagen as your main protein</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Collagen has no tryptophan and very little leucine, so it scores zero for protein quality. A 2020 trial found it did not raise muscle protein synthesis in older women, while whey did. Keep collagen for its specific uses and get your muscle protein elsewhere.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Blaming all whey for lactose trouble</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Whey concentrate contains a few grams of lactose per scoop; whey isolate contains very little. Many people who bloat on concentrate tolerate isolate without issue. Try an isolate before giving up on dairy protein entirely, unless you have a true milk allergy.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Underdosing plant protein</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Single plant proteins contain less leucine and fewer essential amino acids per gram than whey. A 20-gram scoop of pea is not equivalent to 20 grams of whey. Choose a blend, and aim for about 30 grams of protein per serving to close the gap.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting the front label over the ingredient list</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A tub labeled whey isolate can list cheaper concentrate first, and proprietary blends can hide how much of each protein you get. Read the ingredient order, check protein per scoop against scoop weight, and favor third-party certified products.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best type of protein powder?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most people who tolerate dairy, whey protein is the best type: it is rich in leucine, digests quickly, is backed by the most research and is usually the cheapest per gram of quality protein. Whey isolate suits lactose-sensitive people, casein suits bedtime, and a pea-rice blend or soy isolate is the best choice for vegans.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What are the main types of protein powder?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The main types are whey concentrate, whey isolate, whey hydrolysate, casein, milk protein blends, egg white, beef isolate, soy, pea, rice, plant blends and collagen. Dairy and egg proteins score highest for quality, soy is the best single plant protein, blends improve on single plant proteins, and collagen is incomplete.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is whey or plant protein better?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Gram for gram, whey has more leucine and a higher quality score. But training studies show plant proteins such as soy and pea produce similar muscle gains when the dose is adequate. If you eat plant protein, choose a blend and a slightly larger serving. Our whey vs plant protein comparison covers the details.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which protein powder is easiest to digest?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Whey isolate and hydrolysate are generally the easiest for people with lactose sensitivity, since most lactose is filtered out. Egg white is a good dairy-free alternative. Some people find pea protein gentler than whey concentrate. If several types cause problems, check the sweeteners and gums, which are a common hidden cause of bloating.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is casein better than whey?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Neither is better overall; they suit different times. Whey digests fast and is rich in leucine, which suits post-workout shakes and meals. Casein digests slowly over several hours, which suits bedtime and long gaps between meals. Milk protein blends give you both. Total daily protein matters more than which one you choose.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What type of protein powder is best for weight loss?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Whey isolate offers the most protein for the fewest calories, usually with minimal carbohydrate and fat per scoop. Casein can help with fullness between meals. Avoid mass gainers and high-sugar blends. Any protein powder helps with weight loss mainly by making a high-protein, calorie-controlled diet easier to stick to.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are protein blends better than single proteins?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For plant proteins, usually yes, because blends like pea and rice cover each other's amino acid gaps. For dairy, whey-casein blends give a mix of fast and slow digestion but are not superior for muscle. Be wary of proprietary blends that hide amounts or mix in collagen to inflate the protein number.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
